Here's an example of a WordPress plugin that formats order data from WooCommerce into the specified format:

```php
<?php
/*
Plugin Name: Order Formatter
Description: This plugin formats order data from WooCommerce into a specific format.
Version: 1.0
Author: John Doe
*/

function order_formatter_shortcode() {
    $orders = wc_get_orders();
    $formatted_orders = array();

    foreach ($orders as $order) {
        $date = $order->get_date_created()->format('d/m/Y');
        $order_id = $order->get_id();
        $customer_name = $order->get_billing_first_name() . ' ' . $order->get_billing_last_name();
        $total = $order->get_total();
        $taxes = array();
        $items = array();

        foreach ($order->get_items() as $item) {
            $product_id = $item->get_product_id();
            $quantity = $item->get_quantity();
            $price = $item->get_price();
            $tax_class = $item->get_tax_class();

            if (!empty($tax_class)) {
                $taxes[] = "$tax_class, $price";
            }

            $items[] = "$product_id, $quantity, $price";
        }

        $formatted_orders[] = "$date,$order_id,$total,$customer_name,$taxes,$items,$total";
    }

    return implode("\n", $formatted_orders);
}

add_shortcode('order-formatter', 'order_formatter_shortcode');
```

