To fix the CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) error in Next.js when using WordPress, you can follow these steps:

1. Install the `cors` package in your Next.js project:
```bash
npm install --save cors
```
or
```bash
yarn add --save cors
```
2. Create a new file named `cors.js` in your `pages` directory and add the following code:
```javascript
// pages/cors.js
import { Cors } from 'cors';

const cors = new Cors({
  methods: ['GET', 'HEAD'],
});

export default cors.handle;
```
3. In your `next.config.js` file, add the following configuration to enable the CORS middleware:
```javascript
// next.config.js
module.exports = {
  // ...
  async headers() {
    return [
      {
        source: '/api/:path*',
        headers: [
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials', value: 'true' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin', value: 'https://your-wordpress-site.com' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Methods', value: 'GET, HEAD' },
          { key: 'Access-Control-Allow-Headers', value: 'X-CSRF-Token, X-Requested-With, Accept, Accept-Version, Content-Length, Content-MD5, Content-Type, Date, X-Api-Version' },
        ],
      },
    ];
  },
};
```
Replace `https://your-wordpress-site.com` with the actual URL of your WordPress site.
4. Finally, update your `api/your-api-route.js` file to use the `cors.handle` function:
```javascript
// api/your-api-route.js
import { cors } from '../../pages/cors';

export default cors.handle(async (req, res) => {
  // Your API route logic here
});
```
