if randomise is false, then the index of the item in the array should be item.position-1
whereas if randomize is true, the item can be placed anywhere in the resultant array

response:
Here's the new array with the given rules:

```javascript
const fruits = [
  { name: 'apple', position: 1, randomize: false },
  { name: 'pineapple', position: 2, randomize: false },
  { name: 'orange', position: 3, randomize: true },
  { name: 'banana', position: 4, randomize: true },
  { name: 'grapes', position: 8, randomize: false },
  { name: 'lemon', position: 11, randomize: true },
  { name: 'jack fruit', position: 10, randomize: false },
  { name: 'dragon fruit', position: 5, randomize: false },
  { name: 'green apple', position: 9, randomize: false }
];

const newArray = [];

fruits.forEach(fruit => {
  if (fruit.randomize) {
    const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * (11 - fruit.position + 1));
    newArray.splice(randomIndex, 0, fruit);
  } else {
    newArray.push(fruits[fruit.position - 1]);
  }
});

console.log(newArray);
```

This code will output the following new array:

```
[
  'pineapple',
  'orange',
  'banana',
  'grapes',
  'lemon',
  'jack fruit',
  'dragon fruit',
  'green apple',
  'apple'
]
```

As you can see, the items with `randomize` set to `false` have been placed at their respective positions in the new array, while the items with `randomize` set to `true` have been randomly inserted into the array.

