given the input array as above,

create a new array.
if randomise is false, then the index of the item in the array should be item.position-1
whereas if randomize is true, the item can be placed anywhere in the resultant array

response:
To create a new array based on the given input array, we'll iterate through each item and check its `randomize` property. If it's set to `false`, we'll place the item at its desired position (index = item.position - 1) in the new array. If it's set to `true`, we'll randomly select an index for the item within the range of the new array's length. Here's the code to accomplish this:

```javascript
const inputArray = [
  { name: 'apple', position: 1, randomize: false },
  { name: 'pineapple', position: 2, randomize: false },
  { name: 'orange', position: 3, randomize: true },
  { name: 'banana', position: 4, randomize: true },
  { name: 'grapes', position: 8, randomize: false },
  { name: 'lemon', position: 11, randomize: true },
  { name: 'jack fruit', position: 10, randomize: false },
  { name: 'dragon fruit', position: 5, randomize: false },
  { name: 'green apple', position: 9, randomize: false },
];

function generateNewArray(inputArray) {
  const newArray = [];

  for (let i = 0; i < inputArray.length; i++) {
    const item = inputArray[i];
    let randomIndex;

    if (item.randomize === false) {
      randomIndex = item.position - 1;
    } else {
      randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * newArray.length);
    }

    while (newArray[randomIndex]) {
      randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * newArray.length);
    }

    newArray[randomIndex] = item;
  }

  return newArray;
}

const outputArray = generateNewArray(inputArray);
console.log(outputArray);
```

