In ASP.NET Core Web API, you can retrieve the username from the JWT token using the `User` property of the `HttpContext` object. Here's an example:

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;
using Microsoft.IdentityModel.Tokens;
using System.Security.Claims;

namespace WebApiExample.Controllers
{
    [Route("api/[controller]")]
    [ApiController]
    public class ValuesController : ControllerBase
    {
        [Authorize]
        [HttpGet]
        public ActionResult<string> Get()
        {
            var user = User;
            var username = user.Claims.FirstOrDefault(c => c.Type == ClaimTypes.Name)?.Value;

            return $"Hello, {username}!";
        }
    }
}
```

In this example, we use the `Authorize` attribute to ensure that only authenticated users can access the `Get` method. Inside the method, we retrieve the `User` object from the `HttpContext` and then find the claim with the `ClaimTypes.Name` type, which typically contains the username. If the claim is found, we extract the value and use it to greet the user.

