Yes, when the server sets `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` to `\*`, it allows requests from any origin, including cross-origin requests. This can prevent you from including an authorization header with the request, as the browser may not send the header if it believes the request is coming from a different origin.

To include an authorization header with the request, you can use a technique called "preflighted requests" or "OPTIONS requests". This involves sending a preflight request to the server before sending the actual request, which includes the authorization header. The server can then respond with the necessary CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) headers, allowing the browser to send the authorization header with the actual request.

Here's an example of how you can use preflighted requests in JavaScript:
```javascript
const xhr = new XMLHttpRequest();
xhr.open('OPTIONS', 'https://example.com/api/data');
xhr.setRequestHeader('Authorization', 'Bearer my-token');
xhr.onload = function() {
  if (this.status === 200) {
    // Send the actual request with the authorization header
    const data = { /* your data */ };
    const authHeader = 'Bearer my-token';
    xhr.open('GET', 'https://example.com/api/data', true);
    xhr.setRequestHeader('Authorization', authHeader);
    xhr.send(JSON.stringify(data));
  } else {
    console.error('Error:', this.statusText);
  }
};
xhr.send();
```
In this example, we first send a preflight request to `https://example.com/api/data` with the authorization header. If the server responds with a status code of 200, we send the actual request with the same authorization header.

Alternatively, you can use a library like `axios` or `fetch` which handle preflighted requests automatically. Here's an example using `axios`:
