It seems like your server is configured to allow requests from any origin (`Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`) which might be the reason why your client is not able to include an Authorization header.

To enable clients to include an Authorization header, you can modify the server configuration to specify allowed origins instead of using a wildcard. For example, if you want to allow requests only from `https://example.com`, you can set the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to `https://example.com`.

Here's an example of how you can do this in an Nginx configuration file:
```nginx
server {
    listen 80;
    server_name example.com;

    location /api/ {
        proxy_pass http://your-backend-server:8000/api/;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        proxy_set_header Access-Control-Allow-Origin https://example.com;
    }
}
```
In this example, we're configuring Nginx to act as a reverse proxy for our backend server and setting the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header to `https://example.com`. You can adjust the configuration to suit your specific needs.

After making these changes, you should be able to include the Authorization header in your client requests.

