  print("All non-NA elements can be converted to numeric.")
} else {
  print("Not all non-NA elements can be converted to numeric.")
}
```

In this example, we first create a character vector containing some numbers and NAs. Then, we use the `sapply()` function to apply the `as.numeric()` function to each element of the character vector that is not NA. The `all()` function is used to check if all the resulting numeric values are not NA. If they are, then the output will be "All non-NA elements can be converted to numeric.", otherwise it will be "Not all non-NA elements can be converted to numeric."

Here's an R function that checks if all non-NA elements in a character vector can be converted to numeric:

```R
is_all_numeric <- function(x) {
  is_numeric <- function(x) {
    tryCatch({
      as.numeric(x)
    }, error = function(e) {
      FALSE
    })
  }

  !any(is.na(x)) & all(is_numeric(x))
}
```

This function uses a helper function `is_numeric` to check if a single element of the character vector can be converted to numeric. If any element cannot be converted, the function returns `FALSE`. If all elements can be converted, the function returns `TRUE`.

