To configure your Chrome extension's boilerplate files to inject JavaScript on an existing webpage, follow these steps:

1. Create a background script (background.js) for your extension:

```javascript
// background.js

chrome.runtime.onMessage.addListener(
  function(request, sender, sendResponse) {
    if (request.action === 'injectScript') {
      chrome.tabs.query({ active: true, currentWindow: true }, tabs => {
        chrome.tabs.executeScript(tabs[0].id, { file: request.script });
      });
    }
  }
);
```

2. Inject the script from the background script when needed:

```javascript
// content_script.js (or any other script that needs to inject the script)

document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
  chrome.runtime.sendMessage({ action: 'injectScript', script: 'path/to/your/script.js' });
});
```

3. Update your manifest file (manifest.json) to include the required scripts:

```json
// manifest.json

{
  "name": "Your Extension Name",
  "version": "1.0",
  "description": "A brief description of your extension",
  "browser_action": {
    "default_popup": "popup.html",
    "default_icon": "icon.png"
  },
  "permissions": ["activeTab", "tabs"],
  "content_scripts": [
    {
      "matches": ["<all_urls>"],
      "js": ["content_script.js"]
    }
  ],
  "background": {
    "scripts": ["background.js"]
  }
}
```

Replace `path/to/your/script.js` with the actual path to the JavaScript file you want to inject. This setup will allow your Chrome extension to inject the specified script into the current webpage when needed.

