To create a React utility that removes widows, you can use the `React` and `ReactDOM` libraries along with a custom function to check for widows. Here's an example:

```javascript
import React from 'react';
import ReactDOM from 'react-dom';

const App = () => {
  const text = "This is a sample text that may contain widows.";
  const paragraphs = text.split(".");

  const removeWidows = (paragraphs) => {
    const widowedParagraphs = [];
    const nonWidowedParagraphs = [];

    paragraphs.forEach((paragraph) => {
      const words = paragraph.split(" ");
      const lastWord = words[words.length - 1];

      if (lastWord === ".") {
        widowedParagraphs.push(paragraph);
      } else {
        nonWidowedParagraphs.push(paragraph);
      }
    });

    return nonWidowedParagraphs;
  };

  const renderedText = removeWidows(paragraphs).join(". ");

  return (
    <div>
      <p>{renderedText}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

ReactDOM.render(<App />, document.getElementById("root"));
```

In this example, we split the input text into paragraphs using the `split()` method and then check each paragraph for the presence of a period at the end. If a period is present, we add the paragraph to the `widowedParagraphs` array; otherwise, we add it to the `nonWidowedParagraphs` array. Finally, we join the non-widowed paragraphs back into a string using the `join()` method and render the result in the React component.

Note that this is a simple example and may not handle all possible cases of widows. For more advanced widow removal logic, you may need to consider additional factors such as word spacing, line length, and other typographical rules.

To create a React utility that removes widows, you can use the `useRef` hook to store the width of the container element and adjust the layout accordingly. Here's an example:

```jsx
import React, { useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

const WidowRemover = ({ children }) => {
  const containerRef = useRef(null);
  const containerWidth = useRef(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (containerRef.current) {
      containerWidth.current = containerRef.current.offsetWidth;
    }
  }, []);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (containerRef.current && containerWidth.current > 0) {
      const words = children.split(' ');
      const lines = [];
      let currentLine = '';

      for (let i = 0; i < words.length; i++) {
        const wordWidth = words[i].offsetWidth;
        const lineWidth = currentLine + ' ' + words[i] + ' '.offsetWidth;

        if (lineWidth > containerWidth.current) {
          lines.push(currentLine);
          currentLine = words[i];
        } else {
          currentLine += ' ' + words[i];
        }
      }

      lines.push(currentLine);

      const adjustedChildren = lines.map((line, index) => (
        <span key={index}>{line}</span>
      ));

      containerRef.current.innerHTML = adjustedChildren.join('');
    }
  }, [children]);

  return <div ref={containerRef}>{children}</div>;
};

export default WidowRemover;
```

To use this component, simply wrap your content with it:

```jsx
<WidowRemover>
  <p>Lorem ipsum dolor sit amet, consectetur adipiscing elit.</p>
  <p>Suspendisse potenti.</p>
</WidowRemover>
```

