Here are three pivotal moments during World War I:

1. The Battle of the Somme (July 1-November 13, 1916): This was one of the largest battles of the war, fought between the British and French armies and the German Empire. It resulted in over 1 million casualties on both sides and marked a turning point in the war, as it led to a stalemate on the Western Front. Lesser-known factors that contributed to the outcome of this moment include the poor planning and execution of the attack by the Allied forces, as well as the effectiveness of the German defenses.
2. The Russian Revolution (February 7-25, 1917): This was a series of revolutions in Russia that resulted in the abdication of Tsar Nicholas II and the establishment of a provisional government. It had a significant impact on the war, as it led to the withdrawal of Russia from the conflict and the transfer of millions of troops to the Eastern Front. Lesser-known factors that contributed to the outcome of this moment include the growing discontent among the Russian people with the war effort and the weakness of the tsarist regime.
3. The Armistice (November 11, 1918): This was the agreement that ended the fighting between the Allied powers and the Central powers. It was signed in Compiegne, France, and resulted in the surrender of Germany. It had a profound impact on the world, as it marked the end of a devastating conflict that had lasted for four years and resulted in over 40 million casualties. Lesser-known factors that contributed to the outcome of this moment include the exhaustion of both sides, the entry of the United States into the war on the side of the Allies, and the negotiation skills of the Allied leaders.

